FILES AND MECHANISMS

1. EngineNeverWaits
   tests/formal/models.rs::acp_flow now states enabledness of the actual
   EngineEmit action whenever an event is due. The invariant copies that
   action's guard, so a writer-capacity dependency changes the property too.
   BrokenHarnessAcpFlowC makes the actual guard wait on writer_queue < 2.
   A full queue is reachable while another engine event is pending: correct
   states have a real outgoing emit edge; the broken states do not.
   tests/formal_nonvacuity.rs checks those edges and the named safety failure.
   There is no independently maintained bad/waiting flag.

2. MCP group close
   HarnessMcpGroupClose models one leader and zero to two group members.
   Both ignore SIGTERM. Grace expiry delivers SIGKILL, then reaping completes
   Close. NoMemberAfterClose reads leader_alive and members_alive at that
   completed state. BrokenHarnessMcpGroupClose kills only the leader and
   reaches completed Close with a live member. The native witness also reaches
   Close after ignored SIGTERM with the leader and both members still alive.
   verification/contracts.toml maps this model directly to src/mcp.rs::finish_child.
   verification/corpus.toml and coverage.toml inventory the model and control.

3. Admission lag
   src/frontends/acp.rs::await_prompt_admission resubscribes from the actual
   Lagged epoch/resume_after and waits for the prompt's durable caused event.
   The session worker awaits this helper before its next message. It does not
   infer admission merely from queue pressure or unrelated events.
   The unit witness fills the real 4096-slot gateway queue with a valid
   transcript, beyond which the prompt's TurnQueued is durable but absent
   from the old subscription. Replay returns precisely that op's TurnQueued.
   The fixture holds EOF after the actual Lagged frame; without recovery the
   test reaches its named hang guard. check_transcript passes on the fixture.

   Limit of that witness: current InProc normally closes a lagged subscription,
   and the old loop could stop on that EOF. The held EOF intentionally proves
   progress follows Lagged itself and recovery observes the caused event;
   it is not a claim that every existing InProc overflow hangs in practice.
   Production cancellation and ordinary EOF remain handled. The test guard
   bounds a deliberately pending future, not a wall-clock percentile.

4. Leader-only process control and close guard
   scripts/test-e1c-rework-controls.py now replaces both negative-pgid signals
   in finish_child with positive-pid signals. The child member has its own
   stdio handles, so a surviving member cannot mask this failure as a pipe or
   Close-join timeout. The witness requires Close to join, then polls ESRCH
   and fails specifically at "MCP server group survived Close: orphaned member".
   Its failure cleanup kills the group owned by this test.
   The shared tests/mcp_client.rs::close helper now has a 15-second hang guard.
   The previous no-signal control is also rerun and fails its Close-join guard,
   keeping that failure distinct from leader-only cleanup.

   The script accepts --case selectors and --out so these controls can run
   independently without overwriting earlier E1c evidence. Every mutation is
   restored in finally before its green run; an exclusive worktree claim
   prevents overlapping mutation passes.

